why I removed it. Might be a power issue, so when the socket power gets to high, the CPU stalls?

I created an annotation script in python that shows the latency's directly from the trace,
so no need to manually calculate the numbers based on timestamps. evl_annotate_trace.py
also in the attached zip.

What it does: Remember the timestamp / at: value from evl_timer_shot and annotates the
next irq / evl_timer_shot in color. There are some assumptions based on the data:

- evl_timer_shot: [proxy-timer/3]: I did not see any timestamp that triggers this one -> Ignore
- After irq_handler_entry: irq=121 name=proxy tick -> next evl_timer_shot: latmus_pulse_handler
   is not triggered by a evl_timer_shot. So don't annotate it but use it for next irq.

It is just a first draft but helps a lot interpreting the data. If you think this is useful,
it could be improved by adding annotations to all functions that lead to irq_handler_entry.
